{"parentUuid": "7f40cb69-8f0e-42f7-ada9-78a3f81e86dc", "isSidechain": false, "message": {"model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "id": "msg_011ChKYikntfzuL6Amt9DKop", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "这是一篇约 300 词的关于河流的短文（英文，按您的请求）：\n\nRivers are among the oldest teachers of the human race. Long before people wrote down laws or drew maps, they followed rivers, drank from them, and settled along their banks. The Nile, the Tigris and Euphrates, the Yangtze, and the Ganges each cradled early civilizations, because a river offered water, fertile soil, and a highway that needed no road.\n\nA river is never still, yet it is patient. It begins as a trickle from a mountain spring or a melting glacier, gathers strength from tributaries, and carves its path through stone over thousands of years. Gorges and canyons are its handwriting, and deltas are the sentences it leaves at the sea. Every bend tells the story of the land it has pushed against.\n\nRivers also connect what would otherwise be separate. They carry sediment, seeds, and nutrients downstream, feeding wetlands and coastal waters. Salmon swim upstream to spawn, herons wait in the shallows, and otters fish beneath overhanging willows. A healthy river is a living web, and when we dam, pollute, or drain it, that web frays quickly.\n\nThere is a lesson in the way rivers move. They do not fight the obstacles in their path; they flow around them, or wear them down gradually. They always find the lowest ground, and they always keep going.\n\nToday, many rivers run dry before they reach the sea, and many are choked by waste. Protecting them is not only an environmental duty but a way of protecting ourselves, since the water that sustains our cities, crops, and bodies begins its journey in these quiet, persistent channels. To care for a river is to care for the future."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "c